   "directed": true,
   "relationship": "State surveillance of labour and the left from the First World War: the RCMP Security Bulletins (with Whitaker, 1989–97) and archival studies of political policing (1988–92), arguing that 'For the RCMP the Cold War began in 1919, not in 1946' (1992); later syntheses Secret Service (2012, with Whitaker and Parnaby) and Spying on Canadians (2017)",
   "map_label": "Political policing from 1914, 1988–2017",
   "evidence_note": "His own words, in period. 'The RCMP, the Special Branch, and the Early Days of the Communist Party of Canada: A Documentary Article', Labour/Le Travail 30 (1992), pp. 169–204, introduction read (pp. 169–171): documents from the Public Record Office and Access to Information requests to CSIS 'provide considerable insight into the early co-operation between the fledgling Royal Canadian Mounted Police security and intelligence apparatus of the early 1920s and its more experienced partners in England' (p. 169); the weekly RCMP security bulletins 'circulated to Cabinet' were 'routinely sent to the Special Branch at the Colonial Office' (p. 170); 'Such documentation also makes clear how early Canadian intelligence efforts focused on labour radicalism and socialism. For the RCMP the Cold War began in 1919, not in 1946' (p. 171). His n. 4 (p. 170) describes 'the multi-volume Gregory S. Kealey and Reg Whitaker, eds., The RCMP Security Bulletins (St. John's 1989 and forthcoming)', drawn 'primarily' from Access requests to CSIS. The 1988 author note in Class, Gender, and Region (p. iv, read) says he 'is currently working on Canadian state repression of labour and the left'. The 1919 essay (1984, p. 43, read) already draws on an RCMP draft memorandum of September 1919. Title and catalogue level only: 'State Repression of Labour and the Left in Canada, 1914–20' (CHR 73:3, 1992, pp. 281–314) and 'The Surveillance State' (Intelligence and National Security 7:3, 1992, pp. 179–210), both reprinted as chapters of Spying on Canadians (2017; UTP chapter records); Secret Service: Political Policing in Canada from the Fenians to Fortress America (2012), by Reg Whitaker, Gregory S. Kealey and Andrew Parnaby (publisher's description: 'the many tensions that arise when undercover police and their covert methods are deployed too freely in a liberal democratic society'). Relation to the strand: security_state currently holds Whitaker and Marcuse's Cold War Canada (1945–57). Kealey's argument dates the security state's anti-communist focus to 1919; this is an extension in time by Whitaker's co-editor, not a critique of their book. No in-period reviews of the Bulletins were read (Dansereau, RHAF 1995, located only).",
   "source_ids": [
    "pe138_kealey_rcmp_1992",
    "pe138_kealey_bulletins_1989",
