{"node": "urbanhistory", "row": {"person_id": "patricia_thornton", "role": "historian", "context": "Olson's co-author on nineteenth-century Montreal: the demographic analysis of sampled families (credited to her in Olson's 1997 chapter) and Peopling the North American City (2011), which follows families of twelve surnames. The book is known here from publisher copy and the openings of two reviews.", "works": "With Olson, Peopling the North American City: Montreal, 1840–1900 (2011)", "source_ids": ["pe139_olson_peopling_2011", "pe139_olson_peuplement_1997", "pe139_olson_horner_2013"], "basis": "source_review"}, "proposal_id": null}
{"node": "quant", "row": {"person_id": "bruce_curtis", "role": "critic", "context": "Historical sociologist of the census who treats census returns as artefacts of state knowledge: reviewing Darroch and Soltow's study of the 1871 Ontario census (1996) he wrote that 'The census simply cannot tell us much' about how property was valued, gendered or inherited, while calling the book 'an important contribution'; his census history (2001) calls census making 'the making of things to be taken' (as Belshaw quotes it).", "works": "Review of Darroch and Soltow, Property and Inequality in Victorian Ontario (1996); The Politics of Population (2001)", "source_ids": ["pe139_curtis_darroch_review_1996", "lof_curtis_2001", "pe139_curtis_belshaw_2002"], "basis": "source_review"}, "proposal_id": "pe139_curtis_rel_02"}
