Crossref record checked (authors Philip Corrigan and Bruce Curtis; Historical Papers 20:1, pp. 156–171). Unread: Érudit returned its Anubis bot-check page on 2026-10-14 and no further attempt was made. A search-engine summary describes it as asking historians of education to treat public schooling as a state-provided and state-regulated institution, approached through inspection; that summary is not used as evidence.

 pe139_curtis_darroch_review_1996 Bruce Curtis, review of Gordon Darroch and Lee Soltow, Property and Inequality in Victorian Ontario: Structural Patterns and Cultural Communities in the 1871 Census (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 1994), Historical Studies in Education / Revue d'histoire de l'éducation 8, no. 1 (1996): 107–110. https://historicalstudiesineducation.ca/index.php/edu_hse-rhe/article/view/1419
Read in full from the journal's own PDF (galley 1543; scanned, local English OCR; folios 107–110; signed 'Bruce Curtis, Carleton University'). Passages used: 'Anyone who has worked with manuscript census data must be impressed with the meticulous and painstaking analysis undertaken here' (p. 109); 'But I cannot say I enjoyed reading this book. Numbers and narratives do not seem to like one another' (p. 109); 'Two related critical points seem worth making about census artifacts and the matter of gender' (p. 109); the authors 'go so far as to suggest that their cross-sectional study is actually longitudinal' (p. 109); 'The census simply cannot tell us much about the value of distributed property, about the gendered distribution of property, certainly nothing about inheritance patterns, although, as an ideological practice, census-taking attached property to adult men' (pp. 109–110); 'But the book is an important contribution to ongoing debates' (p. 110).

 pe139_curtis_davey_1989 Ian Davey, review of Bruce Curtis, Building the Educational State: Canada West, 1836–1871 (Lewes and London, ON: Falmer Press and Althouse Press, 1988), Historical Studies in Education / Revue d'histoire de l'éducation 1, no. 1 (1989): 161–165. https://historicalstudiesineducation.ca/index.php/edu_hse-rhe/article/view/1360
Read in full from the journal's own PDF (galley 1498; scanned, local English OCR; folios 162–165 from the running heads, the first page being 161 per the OJS record). Passages used: Corrigan's preface, Curtis 'makes us see differently' (p. 9 of the book, as Davey quotes it; the OCR reads 'dif3ferently'); 'one of the great strengths of Curtis' approach is his grounding of Foucault's analysis of power and subjectivity within a marxist theory of state formation and cultural forms' (p. 161); Curtis's argument that 'the construction of the Educational State was accomplished only through the destruction of a prior educational organization and the marginalization of the structure of educational possibilities' (book p. 15, quoted pp. 161–162); 'there is no reference to Gramsci or the theory of hegemony in the bibliography or the index' (p. 162); state schooling as 'the anchoring of the conditions of political governance in the selves of the governed' (no page given by Davey; p. 162); education 'was not a means to government; education was government: government of the self' (book p. 110, quoted p. 163); 'public education was administered into dominance only through a myriad of local struggles' (book p. 132, quoted p. 163); 'My major criticism of Curtis is that he fails to provide an adequate account of patriarchal relations in the period', his framework 'privileges class conflicts' (p. 165).

 pe139_curtis_miller_1993 Pavla Miller, review of Bruce Curtis, True Government by Choice Men? Inspection, Education and State Formation in Canada West (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 1992), Historical Studies in Education / Revue d'histoire de l'éducation 5, no. 1 (1993): 123–126. https://historicalstudiesineducation.ca/index.php/edu_hse-rhe/article/view/1003
Read in full from the journal's own PDF (galley 1145; scanned, local English OCR; folios 123–126). Passages used: Curtis 'makes a significant contribution to history and sociology by utilizing some key insights from the work of Foucault and other theorists, but takes exemplary care with historical evidence' (p. 123); 'Educational inspection was about state formation: the creation, stabilization and normalisation of relations of power, authority, domination and exploitation' (book p. 32, quoted p. 123); 'no central state agency could govern a population about which it was ignorant' (book p. 30, quoted pp. 123–124); 'Bureaucratic administration was seen by political liberals as practical education, a ‘normal school’ for the people' (book p. 7, quoted p. 124); 'Such concerns suggest a social control problematic, and Curtis carefully distances himself from this form of argument', depicting 'an ever-contested and incomplete process of rule' (p. 124); 'My major problem with this excellent book concerns the relative invisibility of gender relations' (p. 125).

 pe139_curtis_palmer_1993 Bryan D. Palmer, joint review of Bruce Curtis, True Government by Choice Men? Inspection, Education, and State Formation in Canada West (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 1992) and Allan Greer and Ian Radforth, eds., Colonial Leviathan: State Formation in Mid-Nineteenth Century Canada (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 1992), Labour/Le Travail 31 (1993): 365–367 (in the issue's 'Reviews / Comptes Rendus' section, pp. 365–433). https://lltjournal.ca/index.php/llt/article/view/4891
Read in full (pp. 365–367) in the journal's own PDF of the reviews section (lltjournal.ca article 4891, galley 5764; scanned pages with a text layer; folios 365, 366 and 367 printed in the running heads). Passages used: 'THESE BOOKS are searching for the state in pre-Confederation Canada'; 'In their purpose they can only be applauded; but their search is at times disturbingly narrow' (p. 365); Curtis 'telescopes the process of state formation in Canada West in the 1840s into the emergence of educational inspectors' and 'this study is too minutely focused' (p. 365); the promotional claim that Curtis 'presents a systematic application of the insights of ... Marx, Weber, Durkheim, Gramsci, and Foucault' (p. 365); as a 'model' Curtis's account 'is overstated to the point of distorting actual historical relations in the evolution of state and civil society' (p. 366); 'the collection lacks coherence' (p. 366); 'The single most important contribution of the articles in this collection, also developed by Curtis, is the notion that the coming of ‘responsible government’ actually entailed the subversion of the reform strategy of the 1830s' (p. 366); the gender essays read as 'complementary to the revisionist assessment of ‘responsible government’ as an expression of a slight [sic] of hand that promised popular sovereignty at the same time as it delivered institutionalized subordination and regulation' (pp. 366–367); 'Books like these make the job easier' (p. 367). Palmer names Greer's chapter on the birth of the police (the text layer prints the name as 'Allan Green's', p. 366).

 pe139_greer_pentland_1985 Allan Greer, “Wage Labour and the Transition to Capitalism: A Critique of Pentland,” Labour/Le Travail 15 (Spring 1985): 7–22. https://lltjournal.ca/index.php/llt/article/view/2454
Read in full (pp. 7–22) in the journal's own PDF (lltjournal.ca article 2454, galley 2857; scanned with a text layer, also OCR'd locally; folios from the running heads). Passages used: Pentland's work is 'a fundamentally incoherent book' and 'neglects the broader context needed to grasp this general transformation' (p. 9); Pentland 'follows the lead of his Toronto mentors in conflating the Canadian economy and its overseas trade' and 'While Innis, Creighton, and their contemporary disciples confine their attention to relations of exchange, however, Pentland at least focuses on the ‘social relations of production’', so 'it seems fair to regard him as the labour historian of the staples school' (p. 9); subdivision of holdings resulted from 'the introduction of domestic industry or of seasonal waged work in the fur or timber trades' (p. 16); Pentland's ethnic argument belongs to 'an ambivalent but, in the end, predominantly liberal, view of the advent of capitalism' (p. 18); 'my own view is that agriculture was organized primarily around the satisfaction of family subsistence needs through family production. There were surpluses and a portion of these were sold, but a significant proportion was diverted to priests and seigneurs through feudal exactions' (pp. 18–19); 'Feudal peasants or market-conscious farmers? No doubt the debates will continue' and 'none of these social formations were dominated by the capitalist mode of production' (p. 19; n. 16 cites Paquet and Wallot 1972 for the commercial view); 'capitalist enterprise had to adapt itself to the prevailing non-capitalist environment' (p. 20); Pentland's neglect of the household economy 'inevitably leads him to write women out of his history' (p. 20); 'If they did, I have no doubt that the aggregate agricultural output would embody vastly more labour than all the furs and squared timbers and other ‘staple products’' (p. 20); subsistence producers 'were therefore scarcely of interest to bourgeois theorists from Ada