 pe139_olson_baltimore_1980 Sherry H. Olson, Baltimore: The Building of an American City (Baltimore and London: Johns Hopkins University Press, 1980), xiii, 432 pp. LCCN 79-21950; ISBN 0-8018-2224-6. Revised and expanded bicentennial edition, 1997.
Front matter read in the Maryland State Archives electronic edition (megafile/ecp/26/037, file olson00.html: copyright page, contents, acknowledgments p. vii, introduction pp. ix–x; the HTML gives no page break inside the introduction, so introduction quotations are cited as pp. ix–x). Chapter text not read. Quoted: 'There is a tempo and a pattern to urban growth'; 'City growth is a boom-and-bust sequence of "long swings," with eighteen years or so separating the neighboring peaks. This applies equally to North America and Europe over at least two centuries, and to much of the world in the last hundred years'; 'This book is about city building, an internal dynamic of the city-state, and how Baltimoreans see themselves and their situation. In that sense, Baltimore is America, in its rhythm of building up and tearing down, swarming and dispersing'; the gaps she names: 'The most serious gap is our ignorance of the lives of slaves and servants in Baltimore', the Polish Catholic community, and 'the strategies of landowners and real estate investors in each round'; 'the city's social record is built into its dwellings' (pp. ix–x). Acknowledgments (p. vii): 'David Harvey shared his discovery of Baltimore for several years'; 'six cohorts of undergraduate students at Johns Hopkins University'. Contents: eleven chapters periodised from 'The Empty Century' to 'Hemmed In, 1935-1979'. The MSA text is a transcription; one OCR capital ('Separating') is silently lower-cased.

 pe139_olson_bonnell_fortin_2014 Jennifer Bonnell and Marcel Fortin, “Introduction,” in Jennifer Bonnell and Marcel Fortin, eds., Historical GIS Research in Canada (Calgary: University of Calgary Press, 2014). Open access, CC BY-NC-ND.
Read in the publisher's open-access front-matter PDF (University of Calgary repository, UofCPress_HistoricalGIS_2014_Frontmatter.pdf), pages from the running heads. p. xii: 'In Canada, the HGIS landscape is very different than in the UK and the United States. Since the 1990s, only a small number of historical geographers and historians have used GIS technology to create geospatial representations of past landscapes and societies in Canada. Notable projects include Montréal, l’avenir du passé, a comprehensive HGIS database of Montreal led by McGill geographer Sherry Olson and Memorial University historian Robert Sweeny'; Gilliland's work 'with Olson on the spatial history of Montreal' has 'made important contribution to our understanding of Canada’s urban history'. p. xiii: 'Olson, Sweeny, and Gilliland’s work has captured the interest of a new generation of graduate students and new scholars', named beside Turkel's digital-history influence and NiCHE. p. xi: the national repositories (the US NHGIS; Gregory and Southall's Great Britain Historical GIS) are 'some of the most important work in the field'. p. xvii summarises Dufaux and Olson's chapter. p. xix n. 12: 'A great deal of scholarship has resulted from this project, among them Sherry Olson and Patricia Thornton’s recent book'.

 pe139_olson_dufaux_2014 François Dufaux and Sherry Olson, “Rebuilding a Neighbourhood of Montreal,” in Jennifer Bonnell and Marcel Fortin, eds., Historical GIS Research in Canada (Calgary: University of Calgary Press, 2014), ch. 8.
Read in the publisher's open-access chapter PDF (UofCPress_HistoricalGIS_2014_Chapter08.pdf): pp. 153, 155, 175–176 and notes 1–2; pages from the running heads. p. 153: the story 'points to the value of HGIS as a research instrument – for the curious citizen, the adventurous amateur, or the advanced scholar'. p. 155: 'the convenient instrument is the HGIS "MAP, Montréal, l’avenir du passé," a collective project in which dozens of people have invested their skills, each with a different purpose in mind'; 'Such studies draw on the massive databases integrated into MAP. The spatial links place the household on the cadastral lot – 7,000 lots in the 1850s, 30,000 in 1900 – with sufficient precision to measure buildings as small as the privy or the stable.' p. 175: 'For MAP, we adopted a rather high level of precision – individual addresses, accurate street widths and buildings as small as the privy – in order to exploit particular sources for a particular city (notably the Goad Atlas of 1881)'; 'essentially a pedagogy of research into the urban habitat'. n. 1 (p. 176): 'A dozen Montreal scholars and a dozen graduate students initiated the project in 2000'; thanks for 'the continuous participation and insights of Robert C. H. Sweeny and Jason Gilliland'.

 pe139_olson_gilliland_claims_1998 Jason Gilliland and Sherry Olson, “Claims on Housing Space in Nineteenth-Century Montreal,” Urban History Review / Revue d’histoire urbaine 26, no. 2 (1998): 3–16. doi:10.7202/1016655ar.
Author abstract only (OpenAlex record of the DOI). The UTP Journals PDF returned a Cloudflare challenge, which was not attempted further; Érudit not tried. Abstract: 'Space per person is a fundamental measure of equity in an urban society. From small samples of the Montreal population over the years 1861-1901, we infer substantial improvement in the average dwelling space available per person, but an extreme and persistent inequity in the distribution among households.'

 pe139_olson_gilliland_segregation_2009 Jason Gilliland and Sherry Olson, “Residential Segregation in the Industrializing City: A Closer Look,” Urban Geography 31, no. 1 (2010): 29–58. doi:10.2747/0272-3638.31.1.29.
Author abstract only (OpenAlex; dated 2009 there, volume 31 no. 1 of 2010 in the DOI record; Lutz et al. cite '33 (January 2010)'). Abstract: 'Taking advantage of an unusual historical database—a historical geographic information system (H-GIS)—we locate 17,000 individual households with precision, and evaluate the "dissimilarity" of neighborhoods along several social dimensions and at various levels of spatial aggregation'; coupling the GIS 'with a statistical model such as the classic index of dissimilarity lends new power to grasp the scale of phenomena ... and even to challenge our assumptions about the meaning of "segregation" or "integration".'

 pe139_olson_hanna_hac_1990 Sherry Olson and D. C. Hanna, “The Social Landscape of Montréal, 1901,” Historical Atlas of Canada, vol. 3 (Toronto: University of Toronto Press, 1990), plate 30. doi:10.3138/9781442675766-037.
Title-level only (OpenAlex/UTP DOI record, plate 30). Olson (2004, Labour/Le Travail, p. 189, in the notes) cites 'Olson and Hanna in Historical Atlas of Canada, Volume III (Toronto 1990), Plate 30; Olson and Hanna in Historical Atlas of Canada, Volume II (Toronto 1993), Plate 49'. Plates not seen; volume editors not checked.

 pe139_olson_horner_2013 Dan Horner, review of Peopling the North American City: Montreal 1840–1900, by Sherry Olson and Patricia Thornton, Histoire sociale / Social History 46, no. 91 (2013): 251–253.
Snippet witness only: the opening paragraphs in the Project MUSE excerpt carried by OpenAlex; the journal's own site returned a bot challenge and was not pursued. 'Historical geographers Sherry Olson and Patricia Thornton have been publishing innovative and insightful articles on demographic change in 19th and 20th century Montreal for over twenty years'; the book 'makes a vital contribution not only to the historiography of Montreal and Quebec, but also to the way that historians and geographers conceptualize the relationship between individuals, families, communities and urban space'; it rests on 'a massive multi-generational sampling that, by mirroring the city's ethnic composition, creates what the authors refer to as "a miniature Montreal"'. The rest, including any criticism, is unread.

 pe139_olson_llt_2004 Sherry Olson, “Ethnic Partition of the Work Force in 1840s Montréal,” Labour / Le Travail 53 (Spring 2004): 159–202 (research note).
Journal PDF (article/download/5341/6210) from the journal's own site; pages from the running heads. Read: pp. 159–165, 173–174 and 192. Quoted: she uses 'the 1842 manuscript census to estimate the relative sizes of four cultural communities and the social distances among them' (p. 159); the census's 'street-by-street organization, in conjunction with the detailed 1846 map of surveyor James Cane, provides cues to residential patterns' (p. 165); a note on p. 165 (n. 32 by the note sequence): 'In a joint program with a dozen scholars, Robert Sweeny (Memorial University of Newfoundland) has created from the Cane map an electronic framework, and we anticipate further analysis of the spatial occupancy'; 'I have in fact cross-referenced the three sources—the census of 1842, the city directory of 1848, and the taxroll of 1848—in an iterative process of matching surnames, first names, streets of residence, and occupations. Of 5,192 residential entries in the rental taxroll, 75 per cent can be matched to the directory, 45 per cent to the census 5 years earlier, and 41 per cent to both sources' (pp. 173–174); 'The research was conceived in collaboration with Patricia Thornton of Concordia University' (p. 192). Lutz et al. (2014, n. 31) cite this article as Labour/Le Travail 53 (2004): 1–66; the journal gives 159–202.

 pe139_olson_lutz_2014 John S. Lutz, Patrick A. Dunae, Jason Gilliland, Don Lafreniere and Megan Harvey, “Turning Space Inside Out: Spatial History and Race in Victorian Victoria,” in Jennifer Bonnell and Marcel Fortin, eds., Historical GIS Research in Canada (Calgary: University of Calgary Press, 2014), ch. 1.
Read in the publisher's open-access chapter PDF (UofCPress_HistoricalGIS_2014_Chapter01.pdf), pages from the running heads. p. 11: 'In practice, we are following the lead of Sherry Olson, whose pioneering work on nineteenth-century Montreal is a model of spatial history and building on the expertise that our team mates developed while constructing an historical GIS of London, Ontario.' n. 31 (pp. 23–24): 'We have benefited from foundational studies by Sherry Olson', listing the 1989 Historical Methods article, the 2004 Labour/Le Travail note, Gilliland and Olson on MAP (GEOinfo), Peopling (2011), Gilliland and Olson on segregation and on housing space (1998). Not independent of her: co-author Jason Gilliland was her collaborator on MAP and co-author of the 1998, 2010 and 2011 papers.

 pe139_olson_map_record Robert C. H. Sweeny, Sherry Olson, Jean-Claude Robert et al., “Montréal, l’avenir du passé (MAP),” Memorial University Research Repository, record 227.
Repository abstract as held by OpenAlex; the repository URL now redirects to the new Memorial repository home page, so the record itself was not seen and its date is uncertain (it describes later layers in the present tense). Abstract: 'Montréal l'avenir du passé (MAP) was established in 2000 to create an historical GIS research infrastructure for 19th and 20th century Montréal. It consists of six highly detailed digitized historical maps representing all buildings in the city for 1825, 1846, 1880, 1912, 1949 and 2000. The first three and last have been geo-referenced and they have been "peopled" by linking at the street-scape (1846) or lot level (1880 & 2000) census returns, tax records, city directories and a wide variety of non-routinely generated sources.'

 pe139_olson_occupations_1989 Sherry Olson, “Occupations and Residential Spaces in Nineteenth-Century Montreal,” Historical Methods 22, no. 3 (1989): 81–96. doi:10.1080/01615440.1989.10594182.
Title-level only (Crossref/OpenAlex metadata: vol. 22, no. 3, pp. 81–96; no abstract; body closed at Taylor & Francis). Lutz et al. (2014, n. 31) list it first among 'foundational studies by Sherry Olson', citing it as 22 (Summer 1989): 81–99.

 pe139_olson_peopling_2011 Sherry Olson and Patricia A. Thornton, Peopling the North American City: Montreal, 1840–1900, Carleton Library Series 222 (Montreal and Kingston: McGill-Queen’s University Press, 2011), xvii, 524 pp.
Not read. Publisher description (as carried by the Choice and De Gruyter records in OpenAlex): 'Sherry Olson and Patricia Thornton use an ingenious sampling of twelve surnames to track the comings and goings, births, deaths, and marriages of the city's inhabitants'; 'Integrating social and spatial analysis'; 'The culmination of twenty-five years of work'. The book's own words appear here only as Sager (2012) quotes p. 3 ('analytical experiment').

 pe139_olson_peuplement_1997 Sherry Olson, “Le peuplement de Montréal,” in Serge Courville (dir.), Population et territoire, Atlas historique du Québec (Québec: Presses de l’Université Laval, 1997), 81–94. ISBN 2-7637-7494-6.
Read in full (pp. 81–94 and the endnotes) in the CIEQ PDF as deposited on depot.erudit.org (dspace bitstream 005921dd; the depot is not the Érudit journal platform and served the file without a challenge); citation, 1997 date and ISBN from the PDF's own 'Pour citer cet article' page. The notes are unpaginated in this version (the PDF moves them after p. 94). Quoted: 'c’est sur Montréal que nous nous appuierons pour illustrer la période qui va de 1850 à 1900. Nous le ferons en utilisant l’exemple local pour ensuite faire ressortir la vue d’ensemble et saisir ainsi, dans une démarche exploratoire, les processus par lesquels la cité se construit'; migration 'Récurrentes de 20 ans en 20 ans' (p. 81); 'Pour la population protestante, notre microéchantillon s’appuie sur dix patronymes' (p. 85); 'parmi les 370 chefs de ménage du Montréal « miniaturisé » de notre échantillon'; trades from the 'rôle d’évaluation' plotted 'à la manière d’une reconstitution cinématographique', in circles every half-kilometre from the Custom House: half the population within one kilometre of the centre in 1861, in the 1–2 km ring in 1881 and the 2–3 km ring in 1901; builders 'telle une vague déferlante, se déplacent vers la périphérie' (p. 89). Note 1: Patricia Thornton (Concordia) is associated 'pour toutes les analyses démographiques' and David Hanna for construction data; 'Ben Johnson et Ingrid Olson ont effectué la cartographie sur ATLAS GIS, et Lilian Lee a fourni l’assistance technique du laboratoire SIG de l’Université McGill'; 'Jason Gilliland a contribué à l’analyse des loyers et de la mobilité'. Note 4: birthplaces of the 1859 baptismal cohort's parents traced through the Drouin marriage index and the Notre-Dame registers.

 pe139_olson_sager_2012 Eric W. Sager, review of Peopling the North American City: Montreal 1840–1900, by Sherry Olson and Patricia Thornton, Canadian Historical Review (2012). doi:10.1353/can.2012.0055.
Snippet witness only: the opening paragraph as given in the Project MUSE excerpt carried by OpenAlex; the review itself was not opened (MUSE was not tried after its verification wall on earlier drafts). Volume/issue not checked. Opening: 'their book is an example of the research collaboratory that has emerged in recent decades: databases shared among many individuals and teams; ideas shared so widely that Olson and Thornton name others whom they consider to be co-authors of specific chapters'; 'The authors modestly claim to offer an "analytical experiment" in connecting "the individual thread of destiny" to "the chorus of hundreds of thousands" (3). There is nothing modest about the questions they pose'. The rest of the review, including any criticism, is unread.

 pe139_olson_spider_1979 Sherry Olson, “Baltimore Imitates the Spider,” Annals of the Association of American Geographers 69, no. 4 (1979): 557–574. doi:10.1111/j.1467-8306.1979.tb01282.x.
Author abstract only (OpenAlex record of the DOI; body closed): 'The long-swing rhythm of urban investment has produced distinctive spaces and forms in the city. The timing and spatial structure of city-building also created a tempo and a spatial matrix for income redistribution, social conflict, and social pathology. Baltimore, Maryland, illustrates the persistence of such patterns for two centuries. The geographical strategies of Baltimore investors, 1865 to 1878, reveal the connections among several types of urban investment and among various parts of the urban landscape.'

 pe139_olson_ssh_2011 Jason Gilliland, Sherry Olson and Danielle Gauvreau, “Did Segregation Increase as the City Expanded? The Case of Montreal, 1881–1901,” Social Science History 35, no. 4 (2011): 465–503.
Author abstract only (OpenAlex). 'we used a geographic information system (GIS) to situate individual census households with spatial precision on 1 of 12,000 lots in 1881 and 30,000 in 1901, so that we could reaggregate them into meaningful districts of different scales and districts with identical boundaries for both years of observation, thereby overcoming the major methodological problems hindering previous comparative analyses'; 'This study also offers methodological cues for comparative studies in other places and periods.'

 pe139_olson_sweeny_2009 Robert C. H. Sweeny, “Rethinking Boundaries: Interdisciplinary Lessons from the Montréal l’avenir du passé (MAP) Project,” Digital Studies / Le champ numérique 1, no. 2 (2009), published 16 June 2009. doi:10.16995/dscn.107. CC BY 4.0.
Read (opening and section 1, 'What is and Why?') in the Internet Archive capture of the journal page (web.archive.org/web/20211019233751id_/https://www.digitalstudies.org/articles/10.16995/dscn.107/); the live site was not requested. Unpaginated HTML. The author note calls Sweeny 'co-director of the historical GIS project "Montréal l'avenir du passé" (MAP)'. Quoted: MAP was 'a collaboration between geographers and historians'; 'Each layer consists of an historical map dynamically linked to a variety of period sources'; 'All the joys and the frustrations of data entry, warping, proofing, linkage, and analysis were shared'; 'The ontology of geographic information systems is antithetical to historical understanding'; 'A GIS mobilizes centuries of cartographic conventions with the latest in computer technologies to ensure the illusion of accuracy'; 'Accurate rectifications required an almost exclusive reliance on property lines as our anchoring, reference points. Property lines were more accurate because they were more important than the built environment'; 'an historical GIS works because it is a-historical'. Later sections not read.

 pe139_perry_carter_2002 Sarah Carter, review of On the Edge of Empire: Gender, Race and the Making of British Columbia, 1849–1871, by Adele Perry, BC Studies 136 (2002/03): 117–119.
Read in full: pp. 117 and 119 in the single-review PDF (OJS 185112, which omits p. 118) and p. 118 in the issue's book-review PDF (OJS 1654). Passages used: 'racially plural, rough and turbulent'; 'a fiction of our own invention' (Perry, p. 201); 'a settler colony where dispossesion [sic] … and resettlement … were intertwined'; 'a valuable Canadian contribution to "Whiteness" studies' (p. 117); mixed-race relationships 'not confined to the fur trade' and the 'happy stories' (Perry, p. 62) (p. 118); 'a sharp disjuncture between colonial discourse and colonial practice'; the North American setting; 'social centrality and political agency' to be 'further developed' (p. 119).

 pe139_perry_forum_2016 Book Review Forum on Adele Perry’s Colonial Relations: The Douglas-Connolly Family and the Nineteenth-Century Imperial World, BC Studies 190 (2016): 89–114. doi:10.14288/bcs.v0i190.188319.
Journal PDF (OJS 188319; pp. 89–114). Read: Ballantyne at pp. 104–105 (Colonial Relations as 'a salutary reminder of the limitations of work that follows Patrick Wolfe and Lorenzo Veracini'); Perry's response pp. 106–108 and 112–113 ('My 2001 book On the Edge of Empire … makes the argument that British Columbia's history is best understood within a comparative framework of settler colonialism, echoing an assumption that undergirds Cole Harris's 1997 and 2002 books', p. 112; 'fur-trade colonialism as a distinct and meaningful practice of empire', p. 113). Clayton's, Dussart's and Devine's reviews skimmed, not used. Later reading only.

 pe139_perry_orlowski_2002 Paul Orlowski, review of On the Edge of Empire: Gender, Race, and the Making of British Columbia, 1849–1871, by Adele Perry, Left History 8:1 (2002): 119–122. doi:10.25071/1913-9632.5487.
Read in full in the Internet Archive capture of the journal PDF (https://web.archive.org/web/20250826032001/https://lh.journals.yorku.ca/index.php/lh/article/download/5487/4682), because the York site serves a browser check; year and DOI from OpenAlex. Byline University of British Columbia. A search-engine summary attributed this review to Greg Dening, whose review of another book ends on p. 119; the PDF shows Orlowski's signature. Passages used: 'the importance of race to the social experience of gender' (Perry, p. 8) (p. 119); 'feminist postcolonial scholars such as Ann Laura Stoler, Robert J. C. Young, and Anne McClintock' (pp. 119–120); 'sharp disjuncture' (Perry, p. 167) (p. 120); 'wages of whiteness', 'binary racial divide', Chinese labourers (Perry, p. 9), 'discourse' never defined, First Nations resistance 'lacking', alliances through mixed-race couples, class 'central to social relations' (Perry, p. 4) given little attention (p. 121); no mention of wage labour for First Nations men; 'a powerful and cogent argument' (p. 122).

 pe139_perry_presence_2001 Adele Perry, “Presence, Absence, and the Writing of BC History,” BC Studies 132 (2001/02): 57–64. doi:10.14288/bcs.v0i132.1608.
Read in full in the journal PDF (https://ojs.library.ubc.ca/index.php/bcstudies/article/view/1608; OJS metadata pp. 57–64). Passages used: the book 'initially inspired not by local experience but by a rich international literature on gender and colonialism' and the 'erasure of dispossession' sentence, which as printed ends 'settler consciousness' without a connecting word (p. 58); Harris's 'struggle with distance' (p. 58 n. 3); Said's 'germinal insight' (pp. 58–59); 'transnational' scholarship and Burton (p. 59); 'The most remarkable pattern of silence probably concerns Aboriginal peoples' and McClintock (p. 61); 'These sorts of problems are solved by revolutions, not by shifts in scholarly technique' (p. 63); Chakrabarty (pp. 63–64).

 pe139_perry_rutherdale_2002 Myra Rutherdale, review of On the Edge of Empire: Gender, Race, and the Making of British Columbia, 1849–1871, by Adele Perry, Labour/Le Travail 50 (2002): 324–326.
Read in full in the journal's reviews PDF for vol. 50 (lltjournal.ca article 5286, pp. 307–378; page heads checked). Byline University of British Columbia. Passages used: 'informed by an international post-colonial, feminist, gendered, and Marxist analysis'; 'At the heart of this imagined community was Whiteness'; colonization and immigration 'in tandem in an attempt to create a White colony'; 'Influenced by the work of Anne Laura Stoler' with Perry's p. 123 (p. 325); the 'imperial panacea' (Perry, p. 4); the 'Home' and 'Away' relationship 'could be further probed'; the book began as a York University dissertation (p. 326).

 pe139_perry_sick_1995 Adele Perry, “‘Oh I’m Just Sick of the Faces of Men’: Gender Imbalance, Race, Sexuality, and Sociability in Nineteenth-Century British Columbia,” BC Studies 105/106 (1995): 27–44. doi:10.14288/bcs.v0i105/106.969.
Journal PDF read (https://ojs.library.ubc.ca/index.php/bcstudies/article/view/969; OJS metadata pp. 27–44; the last page carries illustrations). Read closely at pp. 27–33 and 42–43; pp. 34–41 skimmed. Passages used: the 'scarcity model' and 'volcano theory' (p. 27); 'a quarter-century of inquiry into women's history has produced little' (p. 28); Noel as 'the most clear example' (p. 29 n. 4); 'based on essentialist understandings of both sexuality and race'; fur-trade historians 'explain their frequency as the product of white women's absence'; Van Kirk's 'brilliant account' (n. 8) and p. 173 (n. 11) (p. 30); 'integral … to colonial discourse itself' (p. 31); Wakefield and the colonial promoters (p. 32); 'never determined by gender alone' (p. 42); 'restricted those economic and social possibilities'; 'my analysis minimizes the subjectivity of First Nations people'; 'white discourse and white discourse alone'; Van Kirk's 'pioneering work' (p. 43). Acknowledges an SSHRC doctoral fellowship (p. 27 n.).

