pe139_greer_rel_03 ["Some scholars speak of an “eliminationist” logic driving settler colonialism ... toward the utter destruction of natives who stand in its way", "such “ethnic cleansing” is not the whole story"]
pe139_harris_rel_01 ["slightly more than one-third of 1 per cent of provincial land", "the initial ability to dispossess rested primarily on physical power and the supporting infrastructure of the state; the momentum to dispossess derived from the interest of capital in profit and of settlers in forging new livelihoods; the legitimation of and moral justification for dispossession lay in a cultural discourse that located civilization and savagery …; and the management of dispossession rested with a set of disciplinary technologies of which maps, numbers, law, and the geography of resettlement itself were the most important.", "locates the non-Native settlement of British Columbia explicitly in the context of the expansion of European imperial and commercial power", "the displacement of Indigenous peoples and the remaking of space that was formerly theirs into an immigrant society", "there are very few \"Native voices\" in it - something Harris himself admits", "despite a few examples to the contrary, emerge as victims", "a provocative and important book", "Harris's most political work to date and arguably the most significant contribution to our understanding of colonialism in the province since Robin Fisher's Contact and Conflict (1977)", "Postscript: The Boundaries of Settler Colonialism"]
pe139_harris_rel_02 ["[imposing] a spatial discipline with a profound capacity to modify Native life", "Native power over the Lower Mainland and, to a considerable extent, earlier Native lifeworlds had collapsed", "According to Harris, in British Columbia disciplinary power rested on the land system (Chapters 3 and 4). To me, this is the heart of Harris's argument … the links between colonization and geographical change are most visible", "the colonial construction of space in British Columbia", "became, in a sense, the primal line on the land of British Columbia, the one that facilitated or constrained all others", "very much the historian's geographer", "will be more widely read and appreciated for what it says about the spatiality of colonialism, or what Harris sees as colonialism's \"geographical core\" (xxiv)", "(however unwittingly) a powerful and important programme for a postcolonial historical geography that significantly departs from current critical norms", "a timely and potent model for the critical appreciation of colonialism's geographies rather than geography's empire", "fixates on the formal disciplinary links between geography and empire", "from some potentially fruitful comparative lines of inquiry and a fuller digging into theory"]
pe139_harris_rel_04 ["a remarkable and influential book — Contact and Conflict — [arguing] that Europeans and Natives met as equals on the Northwest Coast, that contact violence has been considerably exaggerated, and that an infusion of wealth created a brief high point in Northwest Coast culture", "though founded on fear and violence, relied on symbolic gesture and theatre"]
pe139_harris_rel_05 ["The emphasis on culture in studies of colonialism tends to obscure other forms of colonial power while making it impossible to contextualize the cultural argument and assess its salience. Rather than focusing on texts, systems of signification, and procedures of knowledge generation, as the colonial discourse literature is wont to do, a fuller understanding of colonial powers is achieved by explaining colonialism's basic geographical dispossessions of the colonized. In so doing, the issue of power is not prejudged", "Although no one body of theory explains colonial power, several theoretical perspectives yield crucial insights.", "neither a close student of the imperial imagination nor a slavish follower of the powerful postcolonial theories", "his suspicion of an international postcolonial literature that, in his view, tends to overplay the hands of culture and discourse relative to other forms of power", "favours (again however unwittingly) a certain postcolonial narrative", "has absorbed a body of ideas about the ascriptive power of colonial discourse … that were initially advanced by Said"]
pe139_harris_rel_06 ["what Harris, following Michel Foucault, calls \"sovereign power\"", "the resettlement of British Columbia turned on \"disciplinary power,\"", "rested on the land system", "is shaped by his reading of a diverse and complex international theoretical literature on power", "\"lets theory loose\" in British Columbia, as he puts it (xvii)", "Power, Modernity, and Historical Geography"]
pe139_harris_rel_07 ["there are very few \"Native voices\""]
pe139_perry_rel_01 ["British Columbia fits within a broader context of European colonialism in that it was a settler colony where dispossesion [sic] of the indigenous societies and resettlement by a newcomer population were intertwined. While this is imperial history, it is not an example of the triumph of imperialism as in this era Aboriginal peoples remained demographically dominant and socially central", "I found Perry's rendering of First Nations resistance to be lacking throughout much of the work, their portrayal emphasizing passivity rather than agency", "a salutary reminder of the limitations of work that follows Patrick Wolfe and Lorenzo Veracini in understanding \"settler colonialism\" as a fundamentally eliminationist project"]
pe139_perry_rel_02 ["the importance of race to the social experience of gender in nineteenth-century British Columbia", "a sharp disjuncture between colonial discourse and colonial practice on this edge of empire", "fails to invoke what Roediger calls the \"wages of whiteness\"", "a binary racial divide that runs through the entire book but did not run through colonial British Columbia", "class relations are central to social relations"]
pe139_perry_rel_03 ["the mixed-race couples in colonial B.C. had much to do with building alliances, sometimes between Hudson's Bay Company officers and the daughters of Aboriginal chiefs, a strategy that is not considered at all in the book"]
pe139_perry_rel_04 ["Edward Said's Orientalism contains the germinal insight that such stock knowledge functioned to inform and serve the centre rather than to understand the various peripheries about which, putatively, it spoke", "positioned within feminist postcolonial scholars such as Ann Laura Stoler, Robert J. C. Young, and Anne McClintock, all of whom she refers to in the book"]

