pe139_brown_ase_1991 https://doi.org/10.2307/482117 Crossref DOI record (Ethnohistory 38:2, 1991, first page 113; last page not given). Only the opening page was read, as the abstract text in OpenAlex; the body is closed. Passages: 'the rather obscure niche occupied by our field, methodology, approach, or whatever it is we do when we do ethnohistory'; 'auto-ethnohistory'; 'a useful handle for some reflections about ethnohistory and ethnohistorians, myself included'; Bill Simmons asked her in August 1988 'if I would be a candidate for president of the American Society for Ethnohistory'. Her predecessors as president, Axtell (1990) and Fogelson (1989), are named.
pe139_harris_clayton_2003 https://doi.org/10.14288/bcs.v0i138/9.1668 Read in full (https://ojs.library.ubc.ca/index.php/bcstudies/article/view/1668). Clayton says he began working with Harris as a UBC graduate student and that some observations stem from conversations with him (pp. 5–6 and n. 1). Passages used: 'the historian's geographer', 'best known to Canadians for his editorship of the Historical Atlas of Canada' (p. 5); methodological essays of 1971, 1978, 1991 and 'The Simplification of Europe Overseas' (1977) (p. 6 n. 3); 'arguably the most significant contribution … since Robin Fisher's Contact and Conflict (1977)' (p. 6); 'the primal line on the land' and colonialism's 'geographical core' (p. 7); 'suspicion of an international postcolonial literature' (pp. 8–9); 'lets theory loose' (p. 9); Said's 'ascriptive power of colonial discourse' (p. 10); 'colonialism's geographies rather than geography's empire' (p. 11). The editorial cites the 2004 Annals essay as in press (n. 8).
pe139_harris_forum_2003 https://doi.org/10.14288/bcs.v0i138/9.1676 Journal PDF read in full (https://ojs.library.ubc.ca/index.php/bcstudies/article/view/1676; OJS metadata pp. 137–163). Harris's précis pp. 137–151; Fiske pp. 151–155; Gibson (a Senior Fellow of the Fraser Institute, per his note) pp. 156–160; Harris's reply pp. 161–163. Passages used: the four sites of the power to dispossess (pp. 137–138); the land system that 'increasingly bounded and channeled their movements' (p. 138); 'two very different stories underlie British Columbia' (p. 140); 'successful settler colonialism' (p. 144); Fiske, 'unsettling in its apparent simplicity' and the powers that 'did not and cannot exist outside of the symbolic realm' (p. 152); Harris, 'I doubt that Jo-Anne Fiske and I have any real disagreement' (p. 161).
pe139_harris_hac_1987 https://mhs.mb.ca/docs/mb_history/20/historicalatlas.html Not read. Bibliographic details (editor, cartographer/designer, publisher, 1987, 198 pp., ISBN 0-8020-2495-5, sixty-nine plates) from MacDonald's review (Manitoba History 20, 1990); the 1988 Macdonald Prize from Clayton (BC Studies 138/139, 2003, p. 5 n. 2). Preface passages are known only as MacDonald quotes them, without page numbers.
pe139_harris_loo_1998 https://doi.org/10.14288/bcs.v0i117.185301 Read in full in the journal PDF (https://ojs.library.ubc.ca/index.php/bcstudies/article/view/185301). Passages used: The Seigneurial System 'set the terms for the debate over the nature of New World societies in this country'; the Atlas as part of his career; colonization as 'the displacement of Indigenous peoples and the remaking of space'; 'Work like this … exemplifies the potential of regional history'; 'international theoretical literature on power' (p. 64); 'following Michel Foucault … "sovereign power"', 'disciplinary power' resting on the land system, Harris's p. 101 'spatial discipline' and p. 102 (p. 65); 'very few "Native voices"', Native peoples 'emerge as victims', connections to colonization 'fade away' (p. 66); class not discussed; 'a provocative and important book' (p. 67).
pe139_harris_macdonald_1990 https://mhs.mb.ca/docs/mb_history/20/historicalatlas.html Read in full in the Manitoba Historical Society's web text (no page numbers). Passages used: quotations of Harris's preface on 'the economic and social circumstances of ordinary life', more attention to 'Atlantic Canada and to the West', 'accord full place to native peoples' and 'we have not succeeded in doing so', 'an atlas may lead research in some ways, but more commonly it must follow', and the Innis paragraph ('As Innis maintained, the pattern of Canada has been taking shape for almost 500 years'); MacDonald on the volume as 'a tribute to the efforts of many individuals', 'masterly production', the dating risk of the prehistory plates, and the Atlas setting 'the standard for many years'.
pe139_harris_napoleon_2004 https://ojs.library.ubc.ca/index.php/bcstudies/article/view/1706 Read in full in the issue's book-review PDF (OJS article 1706, pp. 105 ff.; this review pp. 114–118, page heads checked). Byline University of Victoria. Passages used: 'Informed by Frantz Fanon, Edward Said, Michel Foucault, and E.P. Thompson' (p. 114); summary of the Canadian Geographer symposium (47:1, 2003) and Harris's reply (pp. 114–115); 'recreates, from a settler perspective, BC history'; the 'structural economic problems' of small communities (p. 115); 'a much more textured, dynamic, and human sense of BC history'; 'insects on pins' and 'Cartesian space' (p. 116); Part 4 'somewhat incongruous'; the politics of difference 'nonetheless problematic'; 'Speaking as a First Nations person …'; 'contemporary Aboriginal voices'; the colonial approach to land 'less about legal or moral principles than about the varying self-interests of the colonizers' (p. 117); 'a valuable resource for both Aboriginal and non-Aboriginal peoples' (p. 118).
pe139_harris_social_power_1997 https://doi.org/10.14288/bcs.v0i115/6.1723 Journal PDF read (https://ojs.library.ubc.ca/index.php/bcstudies/article/view/1723; OJS metadata pp. 45–82). Read closely at pp. 45–47, 56–60, 68–70 and 80–82; the sections on inscription and disease skimmed. Passages used: Fisher's Contact and Conflict as 'a remarkable and influential book' arguing 'that Europeans and Natives met as equals' (pp. 46–47); 'the cultural enrichment hypothesis, after a phrase first used by Joyce Wike' (p. 47); traders' 'politics of fear' and Foucault's 'sovereign power' (p. 58); Said's Orientalism as 'The original and most influential discussion' (p. 59 n. 39); 'the cultural enrichment hypothesis needs to be approached very cautiously' (p. 80); 'no fixed datum plane'; 'epidemics, increased violence, depopulation'; 'anything but passive victims' (p. 81). An earlier version was given at the BC Studies conference, Nanaimo, May 1997 (p. 45 n. 1).
