liberal_order_framework transition_to_capitalism Social histories on either side of the transition, read alongside the framework in Canadian PhDs (Bradbury: 13 of 44 citing PhDs also cite McKay 2000, lift 4.0; Greer: 5 of 16, lift 4.2). Greer finds habitant society in the Lower Richelieu, 1740–1840, organized around a feudal order with household self-sufficiency; Bradbury follows Montreal working-class families as wage labour and industrial capitalism remade family survival. Both predate the prospectus and are not checked as responses to it.
cdn_women_gender earlier_women_writers Women wrote Canadian history long before women’s history was a field: popular, local and regional historians (Machar, Skinner, Esther Clark Wright), the independent scholar Isabel Skelton, and a few university historians, among them Hilda Neatby and Margaret Ormsby, the CHA’s first and second women presidents (1962–63; 1965–66). In French Canada, Marie-Claire Daveluy wrote on New France. Most wrote national, local, fur-trade or political history; only Skelton (who “sought to write women back into Canadian history”, per Coulter’s reading of Crowley) and Daveluy (Bienvenue 2018) are documented as writing women in. They are earlier women writers of history, not founders of women’s history. Boutilier and Prentice’s Creating Historical Memory (1997) recovered this work and asked how professionalization masculinized history; the editors note that their subjects come mainly from “the white, Anglo-Celtic segment of society”. Cleverdon’s suffrage history (1950) is the earlier study of women that PhDs still cite. Bienvenue and Dorais (2023) extend the recovery to French Canada (after 2000).
cdn_women_gender comparison_fur_trade_empire Van Kirk’s women in fur-trade society (1980) and Perry’s gender and race in colonial British Columbia (2001) are among the works most cited by this field’s PhDs but are treated mainly in Indigenous–newcomer relations & fur-trade history and Settler colonialism.
native_newcomer agency_as_alibi Brownlie and Kelm (1994) criticised agency-centred work on Indian policy and the potlatch law (Cole and Chaikin 1990; Miller; Loo 1992): stressing Native agency exaggerated how far it became effective self-defence, trivialising the harm of colonial policy and serving as an ‘alibi’ for it. A critique within policy history, not of the fur-trade literature; targets known from a secondary source, essay unread.
native_newcomer indigenous_critiques_of_framing Sioui (1989), Stevenson/Wheeler (1998) and LaRocque (1999) criticise the relations framing from Indigenous standpoints: its reliance on colonial documents, its disregard of oral history and its ethnological distance. Fixico and S. Miller (2009, US) question ‘Indian–white relations’ as the premise of Indian history. Paul’s We Were Not the Savages (1993; the 2000 Fernwood edition is the one PhDs cite), subtitled ‘a Mi’kmaq perspective’, rejects the ‘savage’ category (title and subtitle only). Adams’s Prison of Grass (1975) is an earlier counter-history of official history, not a critique of this field, and Dickason is a member of the field. Trigger (1986) and Brownlie and Kelm (1994) are non-Indigenous critiques from inside. These are critics and alternatives, not members.
cdn_political_national business_economic Bliss's social history of business and his national business synthesis; Drummond's Ontario economic history. Kept as a strand, not a separate group: business history is small and falling (47 PhDs, 4.2% → 3.2%).
cdn_political_national new_political_history Political history as the profession's prizes now recognize it (CHA political history prizes, first listed 2011): Heaman's Tax, Order, and Good Government (2017; best book 2018) on how the fiscal regime was made and received, from a fiscal imperial to a fiscal welfare state; Loo's Moved by the State (2019; 2020) on forced relocation and citizen participation; Bohaker's Doodem and Council Fire (2020; 2021) on Anishinaabe governance through alliance. All after 2000. The citation-based anchors (Granatstein, Bothwell, English) represent the older diplomatic and political synthesis; these books are newer and their uptake is rising (7–12 PhDs each, mostly since 2020).
cdn_political_national francophone_political The CHA's French-language political history article prize (first listed 2012) and its book prize have recognized francophone histories of the Quebec state: Savard on Hydro-Québec under the Union nationale (article prize 2012; book 2013) and Harvey's history of Quebec cultural policy, 1855–1976 (best book 2023). PhD uptake is still low (Savard's chapter 1 PhD and book 5, 2009–2026; Harvey none yet), so they mark recognition rather than use. Bélanger's Laurier (1986) is the pre-2000 francophone political work here. Post-2000 works are later readings.
