cdn_national_tradition row george_f_g_stanley Secondary row (editorial decision, 2026-10-12), kept so that the Riel interpretation of The Birth of Western Canada (1936) is represented; it remains among the most-cited national-era works in the PhD corpus (24 PhDs, 2009–2026). Interpretation not checked here.
cdn_national_tradition row robert_craig_brown Co-author of the Centenary Series volume Canada 1896–1921 (1974), the most-cited national synthesis in this set (34 PhDs, 2009–2026), and Borden's biographer.
liberal_order_framework strand transition_to_capitalism Social histories on either side of the transition, often cited beside the framework in Canadian PhD bibliographies. Greer finds habitant society in the Lower Richelieu, 1740–1840, organized around a feudal order with household self-sufficiency; Bradbury follows Montreal working-class families as wage labour and industrial capitalism remade family survival. Both predate the prospectus and are not checked as responses to it.
liberal_order_framework row bettina_bradbury Follows Montreal working-class families as wage labour and industrial capitalism remade family survival; read alongside the framework in PhD bibliographies, not an adherent.
cdn_working_class strand left_history McKay’s Reasoning Otherwise (2008), first of a planned three-volume history of the left from 1890 to the Winnipeg General Strike; read by many PhDs who engage working-class history.
cdn_working_class row harold_a_logan Institutional history of Canadian unions; the ‘Logan’ of McNaught’s 1981 contrast. Rarely cited by recent PhDs.
cdn_working_class row ian_mckay Historian of the Canadian left: Reasoning Otherwise, the first volume of a planned three-part history, follows leftists from 1890 to the Winnipeg General Strike as they transformed struggles around class, religion, gender and race (publisher’s description). 53% of the 182 PhDs citing McKay (2009–26) engage this field (lift 2.9).
cdn_women_gender scope_note A Canadian research field joining women's history and gender history; the entry does not decide the 1990s argument between them. Quebec women's history (Collectif Clio, Lévesque, Baillargeon) has its primary treatment in Quebec historiography, where 91% of its citing PhDs sit; this entry keeps those authors' work on family, work and sexuality and a critique edge to Quebec historiography. Fur-trade women (Van Kirk) and gender and empire (Perry) are treated mainly in Indigenous–newcomer relations & fur-trade history and Settler colonialism and appear here as comparison rows; sexuality, law and regulation overlap with Moral regulation; childhood and family with Childhood, youth & family in Canada. Palmer's Descent into Discourse is treated in Canadian working-class history, as a critique of the linguistic turn, not of women's history.
cdn_women_gender strand earlier_women_writers Women wrote Canadian history long before women’s history was a field: popular, local and regional historians (Machar, Skinner, Esther Clark Wright), the independent scholar Isabel Skelton, and a few university historians, among them Hilda Neatby and Margaret Ormsby, the CHA’s first and second women presidents (1962–63; 1965–66). In French Canada, Marie-Claire Daveluy wrote on New France. Most wrote national, local, fur-trade or political history; only Skelton (who “sought to write women back into Canadian history”, per Coulter’s reading of Crowley) and Daveluy (Bienvenue 2018) are documented as writing women in. They are earlier women writers of history, not founders of women’s history. Boutilier and Prentice’s Creating Historical Memory (1997) recovered this work and asked how professionalization masculinized history; the editors note that their subjects come mainly from “the white, Anglo-Celtic segment of society”. Cleverdon’s suffrage history (1950) is the earlier study of women that PhDs still cite. Bienvenue and Dorais (2023) extend the recovery to French Canada (after 2000).
cdn_women_gender strand comparison_fur_trade_empire Van Kirk’s women in fur-trade society (1980) and Perry’s gender and race in colonial British Columbia (2001) are among the works most cited by this field’s PhDs but are treated mainly in Indigenous–newcomer relations & fur-trade history and Settler colonialism.
cdn_immigration_ethnicity strand quebec_immigration A small francophone literature on immigration to Quebec: Italian migrants in Montreal and the North Atlantic economy (Ramirez 1984; 1991, in English and French), Chinese Montrealers (Helly 1987), Jewish Montreal between the wars (Anctil 1988), Taschereau's 1988 RHAF essay inviting the field to move beyond community 'ghettos' (title level), and Pâquet on the Quebec state's definition of the immigrant (1997; Tracer les marges de la Cité, 2005, as a later reading). In the 2009–2026 PhD corpus each work is cited by only 3–8 dissertations; Pâquet 2005 is the only one cited mostly at francophone universities (5 of 8). Laval is undercounted.
native_newcomer strand indigenous_critiques_of_framing Sioui (1989), Stevenson/Wheeler (1998) and LaRocque (1999) criticise the relations framing from Indigenous standpoints: its reliance on colonial documents, its disregard of oral history and its ethnological distance. Fixico and S. Miller (2009, US) question ‘Indian–white relations’ as the premise of Indian history. Paul’s We Were Not the Savages (1993; the 2000 Fernwood edition is the one PhDs cite), subtitled ‘a Mi’kmaq perspective’, rejects the ‘savage’ category (title and subtitle only). Adams’s Prison of Grass (1975) is an earlier counter-history of official history, not a critique of this field, and Dickason is a member of the field. Trigger (1986) and Brownlie and Kelm (1994) are non-Indigenous critiques from inside. These are critics and alternatives, not members.
quebec_historiography strand women_in_quebec_history Collectif Clio’s four-century history (1982), Lévesque’s norms and “deviant” women (1989), Baillargeon’s Depression housewives (1991) and her short synthesis (2012). Lévesque (1997) judged that women’s history had redefined traditional Quebec history while some colleagues were slow to integrate women. The anthology places Clio and Micheline Dumont in its modernist part. Primary here: 31 of 34 PhDs citing this literature engage Quebec historiography; Canadian women's & gender history keeps its critique edge (edge_841). The Collectif Clio (1982) was Micheline Dumont, Michèle Jean, Marie Lavigne and Jennifer Stoddart; shared methods or positions are not inferred.
quebec_historiography strand catholic_action_origins Against the Quiet Revolution’s own story of a clerical “grande noirceur”, Catholic Action, personalism and lay movements from the 1930s are read as preparing the 1960s. Two versions (Baum): social democracy (Meunier and Warren) and the family and gender (Gauvreau). Gauvreau judged that Quebec religious history had repeated the Quiet Revolution’s own ideology until these studies. After 2000; moved from Religion, social reform & secularization in Canada: 75 of 85 PhDs citing this literature engage Quebec historiography (lift 5.6), 36 of them at francophone universities.
quebec_historiography row lionel_groulx Author of the national history of French Canada that closes the anthology’s ‘temps des Anciens’; founded the Institut d’histoire de l’Amérique française (1946) and its Revue (1947), contributing to the first issue. Cited by 29 PhDs (2009–26), mostly for his memoirs, as a source.
quebec_historiography row michel_brunet Montreal school; the ‘English presence’ and the two Canadas. Savard: claimed scientific history while long wanting to guide the nation. Cited by 12 PhDs (2009–26), 6 at francophone universities; the Creighton–Brunet comparison is left to the national-tradition relationship note.
quebec_historiography row marcel_trudel Laval historian of New France: a multi-volume Histoire de la Nouvelle-France from 1963 and a history of slavery in French Canada (1960). Savard treats him as distinct from Ouellet despite the label ‘École de Québec’; the anthology places him in its ‘moment de la modernisation’. Cited by 39 PhDs (2009–26), 15 at francophone universities, rising from 2.0% to 4.4%.
cdn_political_national scope_note A research field for the political, diplomatic and national history of the Canadian state, widened to include the 'new political history' of state, tax and governance (Heaman's subtitle) that the CHA's political history prizes have recognized since they were first listed in 2011; business history and the British World are distinct strands. The debate over national history is a separate entry (The 'Who Killed Canadian History?' debate, user decision 2026-10-12): Granatstein's and Bliss's polemics are placed there, their scholarship here; Bliss's main entry is here, with a debate roster row there (user decision 2026-10-12). Military history is a separate entry (Canadian military history); Stacey and Granatstein are shared, not merged. Francophone political history (Bélanger; Savard and Harvey, recognized by CHA prizes but not yet widely cited) is thin in the PhD corpus; Quebec political history belongs mostly to Quebec historiography. Post-2000 works are selected later readings.
cdn_political_national strand business_economic Bliss's social history of business and his national business synthesis; Drummond's Ontario economic history. Kept as a strand, not a separate group: business history is small and falling (47 PhDs, 4.2% → 3.2%).
cdn_political_national strand new_political_history Political history as the profession's prizes now recognize it (CHA political history prizes, first listed 2011): Heaman's Tax, Order, and Good Government (2017; best book 2018) on how the fiscal regime was made and received, from a fiscal imperial to a fiscal welfare state; Loo's Moved by the State (2019; 2020) on forced relocation and citizen participation; Bohaker's Doodem and Council Fire (2020; 2021) on Anishinaabe governance through alliance. All after 2000. The citation-based anchors (Granatstein, Bothwell, English) represent the older diplomatic and political synthesis; these books are newer and their uptake is rising (7–12 PhDs each, mostly since 2020).
cdn_political_national strand francophone_political The CHA's French-language political history article prize (first listed 2012) and its book prize have recognized francophone histories of the Quebec state: Savard on Hydro-Québec under the Union nationale (article prize 2012; book 2013) and Harvey's history of Quebec cultural policy, 1855–1976 (best book 2023). PhD uptake is still low (Savard's chapter 1 PhD and book 5, 2009–2026; Harvey none yet), so they mark recognition rather than use. Bélanger's Laurier (1986) is the pre-2000 francophone political work here. Post-2000 works are later readings.
cdn_political_national row c_p_stacey His history of Canadian external policies (1977, 1981) is the most-cited external-policy work here (35 PhDs, 2009–2026).
cdn_political_national row real_belanger Francophone political biographer of Laurier (Presses de l'Université Laval, 1986). Catalogue level only; few citations in the corpus (2 PhDs, 2009–2026).
cdn_political_national row elsbeth_heaman Tax, Order, and Good Government (2017), subtitled 'A New Political History of Canada', won the CHA's best political history book prize (2018): the fiscal regime and the move from a fiscal imperial to a fiscal welfare state (prize citation). Cited by 12 PhDs, 2009–2026, rising. Also in Liberal order framework and English-Canadian intellectual & cultural history.
cdn_political_national row tina_loo Moved by the State (2019), on forced relocation and 'citizen participation' in postwar Canada, won the CHA's best political history book prize (2020; prize citation). Cited by 12 PhDs since 2020.
cdn_political_national row heidi_bohaker Doodem and Council Fire (2020), on Anishinaabe governance through alliance, won the CHA's best political history book prize (2021; prize citation). Cited by 7 PhDs since 2020.
cdn_regional scope_note A research field for Canada's regions (the Prairie West, British Columbia, the Atlantic provinces including Acadia, and Ontario) and the limited-identities argument. Provincial syntheses, regional economic history, rural history and regional identity are distinct strands. Indigenous and settler-colonial histories of the West and British Columbia overlap strongly (Carter, Harris) and are primarily placed in Indigenous–newcomer relations & fur-trade history, Settler colonialism and environment. Ontario has a strand of provincial syntheses and economic histories chosen by PhD citation counts; Acadia has its own Atlantic strand. The North and Newfoundland are small in the PhD corpus (about 18 and 9 titles) and get only this note. Quebec's regional history appears only as a contrast of scale. Roster inclusion does not imply that historians accepted "limited identities".
cdn_regional strand acadia Acadia before and after the expulsion of 1755. In the PhD corpus this is a substantial and rising literature: 101 of 1,303 PhDs (2009–2026) cite Acadian history works, 5.1% in 2009–14 and 8.9% in 2020–26, 23 of them at francophone universities. Griffiths, an authority on pre-1755 Acadia, wrote on its contexts to 1784 (1992) and the emergence of Acadian identity (2005); Plank (2001) and Faragher (2005) studied the British campaign against the peoples of Acadia and the expulsion; Reid, Basque and colleagues read the 1710 conquest from imperial, Acadian and Mi'kmaw perspectives (2004). Landry and Lang's Histoire de l'Acadie (2001) is a francophone university synthesis of Maritime Acadia over four centuries that sets out to recount the experience of the whole Acadian people, not only its elite. Most of these works are later readings, after 2000. Readers of this literature also engage Quebec historiography (lift 3.2): see the comparison with Quebec historiography.
cdn_regional strand ontario Provincial history of Ontario: Craig's Upper Canada volume in the Canadian Centenary Series (1963); Nelles on the provincial state and the development of forests, mines and hydro-electric power (1974); economic histories of the province (Drummond 1987, Ontario Historical Studies Series) and of Upper Canada (McCalla 1993); Baskerville's concise history (2005) as a later synthesis. Ontario is the province most often named in 2009–2026 History PhD titles (78 of 1,303 match 'Ontario' or 'Toronto'; 6.6% in 2009–14, 3.7% in 2020–26). Works were chosen by PhD citation counts (Nelles 34, McCalla 26, Craig 17, Drummond 15, Baskerville 12) and checked against catalogue and DOI records; no checked source frames Ontario within the limited-identities debate.
cdn_regional row john_mack_faragher An American historian whose narrative of the expulsion of the Acadians (2005) is the most-cited Acadian work in the 2009–2026 PhD corpus (10 PhDs; title level).
settler_colonialism scope_note A selective research field whose theoretical consolidation came mostly after 2000; the entry covers its roots through 2000 and cites selected later works as readings. Settler-colonial theory, Indigenous political thought, Canadian histories of dispossession and comparative imperial history have different genealogies. Roster inclusion is not adoption of the paradigm: Carter, Perry, Harris and Daschuk wrote earlier or beside it. No single founder, origin or Canadian consensus is asserted. Critics appear as critics. Francophone uptake in the PhD corpus is near zero.
settler_colonialism strand health_starvation Co-citation places Daschuk's Plains history next to Wolfe and Veracini (19 of 58 Wolfe/Veracini-citing PhDs). Its own framing is unchecked, so this is a comparison strand.
