136:   "description": "A historical sociologist (Carleton University) whose studies of schooling, school inspection and the census in the Canadas read administration as a means of rule. In Building the Educational State (1988), as a reviewer quotes it, education 'was not a means to government; education was government: government of the self' (p. 110), and the educational state was built 'only through the destruction of a prior educational organization' (p. 15); in True Government by Choice Men? (1992) 'Educational inspection was about state formation: the creation, stabilization and normalisation of relations of power, authority, domination and exploitation' (p. 32). His census history (2001) holds that 'Census making ... is not in any simple sense the taking of things as they are; in an important sense it is the making of things to be taken' (p. 305, as Belshaw quotes it), and in a 1996 review he wrote that 'The census simply cannot tell us much about the value of distributed property, about the gendered distribution of property, certainly nothing about inheritance patterns'. Reviewers credited his joining of Foucault's analysis of power to a Marxist theory of state formation but found gender relations missing (Davey 1989; Miller 1993), and Bryan Palmer judged the inspection study, taken as a model, 'overstated to the point of distorting actual historical relations in the evolution of state and civil society' (1993). In the debate over Ian McKay's liberal order framework, Nancy Christie cites 'Bruce Curtis's important point that what McKay is really describing is governmentality' (2009).",
159:   "date_label": "“Fur-Trade Labour” 1981 · “A Critique of Pentland” and Peasant, Lord, and Merchant 1985 · Colonial Leviathan (ed. with Radforth) 1992 · The Patriots and the People 1993 · Property and Dispossession 2018 · “Settler Colonialism and Beyond” 2019",
186:   "date_label": "The Seigneurial System in Early Canada 1966 · Historical Atlas of Canada, vol. 1 (editor) 1987 · The Resettlement of British Columbia 1997 · ‘Social Power and Cultural Change in Pre-Colonial British Columbia’ 1997–98 · Making Native Space 2002 · ‘Revisiting the Native Land Question’ 2003 · ‘How Did Colonialism Dispossess?’ 2004 · A Bounded Land 2020",
213:   "date_label": "‘Oh I’m Just Sick of the Faces of Men’ 1995 · On the Edge of Empire 2001 · ‘Presence, Absence, and the Writing of BC History’ 2001 · ‘Women, Racialized People, and the Making of the Liberal Order’ 2009 · Colonial Relations 2015",
282:   "proposal_id": "pe139_vankirk_rel_02"
322:   "proposal_id": "pe139_perry_rel_01"
409:   "evidence_note": "His own words, in period. 'The RCMP, the Special Branch, and the Early Days of the Communist Party of Canada: A Documentary Article', Labour/Le Travail 30 (1992), pp. 169–204, introduction read (pp. 169–171): documents from the Public Record Office and Access to Information requests to CSIS 'provide considerable insight into the early co-operation between the fledgling Royal Canadian Mounted Police security and intelligence apparatus of the early 1920s and its more experienced partners in England' (p. 169); the weekly RCMP security bulletins 'circulated to Cabinet' were 'routinely sent to the Special Branch at the Colonial Office' (p. 170); 'Such documentation also makes clear how early Canadian intelligence efforts focused on labour radicalism and socialism. For the RCMP the Cold War began in 1919, not in 1946' (p. 171). His n. 4 (p. 170) describes 'the multi-volume Gregory S. Kealey and Reg Whitaker, eds., The RCMP Security Bulletins (St. John's 1989 and forthcoming)', drawn 'primarily' from Access requests to CSIS. The 1988 author note in Class, Gender, and Region (p. iv, read) says he 'is currently working on Canadian state repression of labour and the left'. The 1919 essay (1984, p. 43, read) already draws on an RCMP draft memorandum of September 1919. Title and catalogue level only: 'State Repression of Labour and the Left in Canada, 1914–20' (CHR 73:3, 1992, pp. 281–314) and 'The Surveillance State' (Intelligence and National Security 7:3, 1992, pp. 179–210), both reprinted as chapters of Spying on Canadians (2017; UTP chapter records); Secret Service: Political Policing in Canada from the Fenians to Fortress America (2012), by Reg Whitaker, Gregory S. Kealey and Andrew Parnaby (publisher's description: 'the many tensions that arise when undercover police and their covert methods are deployed too freely in a liberal democratic society'). Relation to the strand: security_state currently holds Whitaker and Marcuse's Cold War Canada (1945–57). Kealey's argument dates the security state's anti-communist focus to 1919; this is an extension in time by Whitaker's co-editor, not a critique of their book. No in-period reviews of the Bulletins were read (Dansereau, RHAF 1995, located only).",
413:   "proposal_id": "pe138_kealey_rel_04"
513:   "proposal_id": "pe139_vankirk_rel_02"
596:   "evidence_note": "In-period, her own words. The 1983 essay (Canadian Journal of Native Studies 3:1, read in full) reads fur-trade sources through anthropological models. Bishop and Krech's argument for early subarctic 'matriorganization' leads her to ask 'whether native women in emergent metis groups looked for and found ways to maintain this organizational bias in their own families and social lives' (p. 40). She tests Asch's 'unilocality' among the Slavey against fur-trade families (p. 42), using the HBC district report on Île-à-la-Crosse of 1823 (p. 42) and the published Red River testimonies of 1818 (pp. 43–44). She presents women's studies as 'one important perspective, among several' (p. 40). Her presidential address to the American Society for Ethnohistory (Ethnohistory 38:2, 1991) is read only in its opening page, as OpenAlex's abstract text. It speaks of 'the rather obscure niche occupied by our field, methodology, approach, or whatever it is we do when we do ethnohistory', and offers 'auto-ethnohistory' as 'a useful handle for some reflections about ethnohistory and ethnohistorians, myself included' (p. 113). Title-level: The Orders of the Dreamed (with Brightman, 1988), an edition of the trader George Nelson's 1823 account of Cree and Northern Ojibwa religion and myth, and Reading Beyond Words: Contexts for Native History (ed. with Vibert, 1996). Reception, read in full: Fiske (Atlantis 25:1, 2000) cites Brown and Vibert (1996) for the literary turn 'that subjects both oral and written narratives to textual analysis' (p. 13). McPherson (2000, n. 31) cites an essay in that volume as discursive analysis of a Red River trial. Gresko (1981) objected to the 'bits of anthropological theory' in Strangers in Blood (p. 113). Later readings, labelled. Brown (2012, read in the 2017 reprint) recalls that 'my courses and professors directed me towards broader issues of method and theory'. She also says she 'benefited from confronting the multiple problems around defining such categories as \"marriage,\" \"custom,\" and \"society\"' (pp. 191–92). The 2017 part introduction calls the pair 'I coming from anthropology and Sylvia from history' (p. 159). Her essay 'The Blind Men and the Elephant' (1990) is read only in its revised 2017 reprint, which cites a 2014 book. There she asks historians to 'start by deconstructing' the fur trade, and warns that subsuming northern Native history under 'the fur trade era' restricts it to 'certain rather specialized \"Indian-White relations\"' (pp. 63–65); the 1990 wording is unchecked. The edge claims a contribution to the field's documentary method, not that she shaped ethnohistory as a whole.",
623:   "evidence_note": "In-period, her own words. 'Le peuplement de Montréal' (Atlas historique du Québec, 1997; read in full) works from 'l’exemple local pour ensuite faire ressortir la vue d’ensemble et saisir ainsi, dans une démarche exploratoire, les processus par lesquels la cité se construit' (p. 81). Its samples are built from records: the 1859 baptismal cohort's parents traced through the Drouin index and the Notre-Dame registers (n. 4); 'notre microéchantillon s’appuie sur dix patronymes' (p. 85); a 'Montréal « miniaturisé »' of 370 household heads in 1900, and building tradesmen from the 'rôle d’évaluation' plotted in half-kilometre rings 'à la manière d’une reconstitution cinématographique' (p. 89). Note 1 credits the demographic analysis to Patricia Thornton and the mapping 'sur ATLAS GIS' to Ben Johnson and Ingrid Olson, with the McGill GIS laboratory. Earlier work is known only at title level ('Occupations and Residential Spaces', Historical Methods, 1989) or from an abstract (with Gilliland, Urban History Review, 1998: 'From small samples of the Montreal population over the years 1861-1901'). After 2000, her own words. In 2004 she describes the method and its limits: cross-referencing the 1842 census, the 1848 directory and the 1848 taxroll 'in an iterative process of matching surnames, first names, streets of residence, and occupations'; of 5,192 taxroll entries '75 per cent can be matched to the directory, 45 per cent to the census 5 years earlier, and 41 per cent to both sources' (Labour/Le Travail 53, pp. 173–74). In the same note Robert Sweeny 'has created from the Cane map an electronic framework' (p. 165, note). With Dufaux (2014): MAP is 'a collective project in which dozens of people have invested their skills'; 'The spatial links place the household on the cadastral lot – 7,000 lots in the 1850s, 30,000 in 1900' (p. 155); 'A dozen Montreal scholars and a dozen graduate students initiated the project in 2000' (p. 176 n. 1). From abstracts: the 2010 and 2011 papers with Gilliland (and Gauvreau) locate '17,000 individual households' in 1881 and re-aggregate lots into 'districts with identical boundaries for both years', 'overcoming the major methodological problems hindering previous comparative analyses'. Reception (later readings, labelled). Bonnell and Fortin (2014, p. xii): 'Since the 1990s, only a small number of historical geographers and historians have used GIS technology' in Canada; 'Notable projects include Montréal, l’avenir du passé, a comprehensive HGIS database of Montreal led by McGill geographer Sherry Olson and Memorial University historian Robert Sweeny'; 'Olson, Sweeny, and Gilliland’s work has captured the interest of a new generation of graduate students and new scholars' (p. xiii). Lutz, Dunae, Gilliland, Lafreniere and Harvey (2014, p. 11): 'we are following the lead of Sherry Olson, whose pioneering work on nineteenth-century Montreal is a model of spatial history'; their n. 31 lists her 'foundational studies'. That chapter is not independent: Gilliland was her MAP collaborator. Contested: Sweeny, MAP's co-director (Digital Studies, 2009), argues that 'The ontology of geographic information systems is antithetical to historical understanding', that a GIS ensures 'the illusion of accuracy', and that 'an historical GIS works because it is a-historical'. This is a critique of the instrument from inside the project, not of Olson. Not a founding: the editors give the US and British national repositories as 'some of the most important work in the field' (p. xi), and the strand's Gregory and Southall (1998) is a separate, aggregate-boundary tradition. 'Pioneering' is the Victoria team's word, not this entry's.",
688:   "evidence_note": "This arrow represents Curtis's 1996 review of Gordon Darroch and Lee Soltow's census study of property in Ontario (1994) and his census history (2001); it is not a verdict on quantitative history as a whole, and Darroch and Soltow are not in the atlas. His own words (Historical Studies in Education 8, 1996, pp. 107–110, read in full): 'Anyone who has worked with manuscript census data must be impressed with the meticulous and painstaking analysis undertaken here' (p. 109); 'Two related critical points seem worth making about census artifacts and the matter of gender' (p. 109); the authors 'go so far as to suggest that their cross-sectional study is actually longitudinal' (p. 109); 'The census simply cannot tell us much about the value of distributed property, about the gendered distribution of property, certainly nothing about inheritance patterns, although, as an ideological practice, census-taking attached property to adult men' (pp. 109–110); and, qualifying, 'the book is an important contribution to ongoing debates' (p. 110). The census book (2001) is unread; as Belshaw quotes it (2002, read), 'Census making ... is not in any simple sense the taking of things as they are; in an important sense it is the making of things to be taken' (book p. 305), and it 'seeks to tie individuals within an administrative grid and then to hold them steady so that they may become objects of knowledge and government' (book p. 26). Belshaw's reading, in his words: 'Historical demographers know that the census is far from perfectly reliable but, after reading Bruce Curtis’ latest book, many more will be mindful of how political it is throughout', and 'demographic and social historians may ignore Curtis’ findings at their peril'. Kind: critique, because the 1996 text objects to inferences drawn from census returns; the editor may prefer comparison, since Curtis does not reject quantitative work and calls the book he reviews an important contribution.",
773:   "relationship": "British Columbia's reserve system explained as geographical dispossession: a capacity resting on state-backed force, a momentum from capital and settlers, a legitimation in a discourse of civilization and savagery, and a management by 'disciplinary technologies' of maps, numbers, law and the land system (1997–2004); written in a settler society's terms ('successful settler colonialism', 2003) but not from Wolfe's theory, and faulted by reviewers for few Native voices",
788:   "evidence_note": "His own words. 'Revisiting the Native Land Question', BC Studies 138/139 (2003), pp. 137–151, his précis of Making Native Space, read in full: the reserves comprised 'slightly more than one-third of 1 per cent of provincial land' (p. 137); 'The capacity to dispossess rested on raw firepower authorized, usually, by the administrative superstructure of the state. … The momentum to dispossess came from capital seeking profits and from settlers seeking livelihoods' (p. 137); 'The legitimation of dispossession lay with a cultural discourse that located civilization and savagery … the implementation of dispossession rested with a set of disciplinary technologies among which maps, numbers, and, especially, the common law were essential' (p. 138); 'two very different stories underlie British Columbia. One is about progress and development … It rests, however, on the other basic story about this place, which is about dispossession, loss, marginalization, and the struggle for survival' (p. 140); 'Given the values and benefits associated with successful settler colonialism, many British Columbians will find this an exceedingly difficult commitment' (p. 144, of a politics of difference). The 2004 abstract (publisher's abstract via OpenAlex; article unread) states the same four conclusions: 'the initial ability to dispossess rested primarily on physical power and the supporting infrastructure of the state; the momentum to dispossess derived from the interest of capital in profit and of settlers in forging new livelihoods; the legitimation of and moral justification for dispossession lay in a cultural discourse that located civilization and savagery …; and the management of dispossession rested with a set of disciplinary technologies of which maps, numbers, law, and the geography of resettlement itself were the most important.' In-period reception. Tina Loo, review of The Resettlement of British Columbia, BC Studies 117 (1998), pp. 64–67, read in full: Harris 'locates the non-Native settlement of British Columbia explicitly in the context of the expansion of European imperial and commercial power' and sees colonization as 'the displacement of Indigenous peoples and the remaking of space that was formerly theirs into an immigrant society' (p. 64); but 'there are very few \"Native voices\" in it - something Harris himself admits', and Native peoples, 'despite a few examples to the contrary, emerge as victims' (p. 66); in the later chapters 'the connections to colonization … increasingly fade away' and class is not discussed (pp. 66–67); still 'a provocative and important book' (p. 67). Daniel Clayton, BC Studies 138/139 (2003), pp. 5–11, read in full: 'Harris's most political work to date and arguably the most significant contribution to our understanding of colonialism in the province since Robin Fisher's Contact and Conflict (1977)' (p. 6). Jo-Anne Fiske, commentary in the 2003 forum (pp. 151–155, read): he 'says little … of how a change in mindset and a marshalling of political will might be achieved', and what he says of the symbolic 'is unsettling in its apparent simplicity' (p. 152); the powers he describes 'did not and cannot exist outside of the symbolic realm' (p. 152). Harris replied: 'I doubt that Jo-Anne Fiske and I have any real disagreement' (p. 161). Val Napoleon (2004): see pe139_harris_rel_07. Not derived from Wolfe: the texts read cite Merivale, Kymlicka and Macklem, not Wolfe; the settler_colonialism roster already says Harris wrote 'earlier or beside' the paradigm. Later reading, labelled: Perry (BC Studies 190, 2016, p. 112) says her 2001 book read British Columbia 'within a comparative framework of settler colonialism, echoing an assumption that undergirds Cole Harris's 1997 and 2002 books'. A Bounded Land (2020), with its 'Postscript: The Boundaries of Settler Colonialism', is cited at title level only.",
885:   "evidence_note": "This arrow represents Val Napoleon's review (BC Studies 141, Spring 2004, pp. 114–118, read in full), not a verdict of the Indigenous history field, whose roster also lists Harris as a historian. Her criticisms: 'Harris recreates, from a settler perspective, BC history as it unfolded for roughly a century' (p. 115); Part 4, 'Land and Livelihood', is 'somewhat incongruous with the rest of the book' because there he moves from the archival record to argument (p. 117); the politics of difference is 'nonetheless problematic' (p. 117), and 'Speaking as a First Nations person, I do not believe that it matters whether Aboriginal people are the same as everyone else; we still have a special place in Canada' (p. 117); 'In my view, where historical enquiry gives way to thought about the future, Harris should have incorporated contemporary Aboriginal voices into his discussion … It is from within the experience of this conflicted and contradictory milieu that strategies for future change must be developed and tested, not from without' (p. 117); he does not address 'the structural economic problems that small communities are encountering' (p. 115). Her praise, kept: the book gave her 'a much more textured, dynamic, and human sense of BC history' (p. 116); 'What I appreciate most … is Harris's argument that the colonial approach to land was less about legal or moral principles than about the varying self-interests of the colonizers' (p. 117); it is 'a valuable resource for both Aboriginal and non-Aboriginal peoples', and she has 'recommended the book to people from several different Aboriginal communities' (p. 118). She also summarises, without quotation, the Canadian Geographer review symposium of five geographers (47:1, 2003, unread here): they suggested that Harris had '(1) appropriated the voices of patriarchy and of the colonizer' and '(5) insufficiently considered Aboriginal resistance to the reserve allocation system' (pp. 114–115), and Harris replied that 'A book … can only do so much' and 'remains unconvinced that the basic settler attitudes were gendered' (p. 115, her words). Loo (1998, p. 66, read) had made the related point that 'there are very few \"Native voices\"' in the 1997 book (edge 1). Optional: a field-to-person critique naming its proxy, the pattern used for quebec_historiography → fernand_ouellet.",
906:   "evidence_note": "Her own words, in period. 'Presence, Absence, and the Writing of BC History', BC Studies 132 (2001/02), pp. 57–64, read in full: On the Edge of Empire 'was initially inspired not by local experience but by a rich international literature on gender and colonialism. That through reading about imperialism elsewhere I came to see imperialism where I was is a suggestive comment on the convenient erasure of dispossession' (p. 58; the sentence ends 'settler consciousness' without a connecting word); 'The most remarkable pattern of silence probably concerns Aboriginal peoples' (p. 61). The 1995 essay (pp. 42–43, read) calls British Columbia 'a racially divided one controlled by a minority white population constantly engaged in a process of colonization' (p. 42). The book is unread; reviewers quote it: 'In contemporary newspapers and conversations, White British Columbians often long for the days when our society was unquestionably British … When we do so we long for a fiction of our own invention' (p. 201, as quoted by Carter, p. 117). In-period reviews. Sarah Carter, BC Studies 136 (2002/03), pp. 117–119, read in full: 'British Columbia fits within a broader context of European colonialism in that it was a settler colony where dispossesion [sic] of the indigenous societies and resettlement by a newcomer population were intertwined. While this is imperial history, it is not an example of the triumph of imperialism as in this era Aboriginal peoples remained demographically dominant and socially central' (p. 117); but 'The theme of the \"social centrality and political agency\" of the Aboriginal peoples of British Columbia could have been further developed' (p. 119). Myra Rutherdale, Labour/Le Travail 50 (2002), pp. 324–326, read in full: Perry shows 'how colonization and immigration worked in tandem in an attempt to create a White colony, while at the same time deliberately marginalizing the majority of the population, the Aboriginal peoples' (p. 325). Paul Orlowski, Left History 8:1 (2002), pp. 119–122, read in full: 'I found Perry's rendering of First Nations resistance to be lacking throughout much of the work, their portrayal emphasizing passivity rather than agency' (p. 121). Her own caution (1995, p. 43): 'my analysis minimizes the subjectivity of First Nations people'. Not from Wolfe: the 2001 essay cites Said, McClintock, Burton and Chakrabarty, not Wolfe, and the settler_colonialism roster already says Perry wrote 'earlier or beside' the paradigm. Later readings, labelled: in the BC Studies 190 forum (2016) she says the 2001 book 'makes the argument that British Columbia's history is best understood within a comparative framework of settler colonialism, echoing an assumption that undergirds Cole Harris's 1997 and 2002 books' (p. 112); Tony Ballantyne (2016, pp. 104–105) reads Colonial Relations (2015) as 'a salutary reminder of the limitations of work that follows Patrick Wolfe and Lorenzo Veracini in understanding \"settler colonialism\" as a fundamentally eliminationist project'.",
910:   "proposal_id": "pe139_perry_rel_01"
949:   "evidence_note": "Her own words, in period. 1995 (BC Studies 105/106, read at p. 30): 'Historians of the fur trade, while concerned with establishing the salience of mixed-race relationships, nonetheless explain their frequency as the product of white women's absence'; n. 8: 'For a brilliant account which ultimately suggests that the existence of mixed-race relationships was the result of white women's absence from the fur trade, see Sylvia Van Kirk, \"Many Tender Ties\" … Also see Jennifer S. H. Brown, Strangers in Blood … and Robin Fisher, Contact and Conflict'; n. 11 quotes Van Kirk: 'Had it been possible for fur traders to bring their wives out at an earlier date, it is doubtful whether intermarriage with native women would have been so extensive' (Many Tender Ties, p. 173); the 'volcano theory' 'holds that mixed-race relationships are at worst unfathomable and at best the products of abnormal historical circumstances' (p. 30). Qualifications kept: the criticism is of one explanation, not of the field; she calls Van Kirk's account 'brilliant' (p. 30 n. 8) and 'pioneering' (p. 43), and relies on it for the displacement of Aboriginal women (p. 43). 2001: the book is unread; Carter (BC Studies 136, p. 118, read) reports that 'Perry does not write a history of these relationships, noting the difficulties with available sources, but she does argue that they were not confined to the fur trade and could be found in the city, in the country, and in mining towns', and that she 'stresses the need to look beyond the \"happy stories,\" such as the long marriage of James Douglas and Amelia Connolly, that can \"obscure both coercive details and the larger brutality of colonialism\" (62)'. In-period counterpoint: Orlowski (2002, p. 121, read) faults the book because 'the mixed-race couples in colonial B.C. had much to do with building alliances, sometimes between Hudson's Bay Company officers and the daughters of Aboriginal chiefs, a strategy that is not considered at all in the book'. No reply by Van Kirk or Brown was checked. Later reading, labelled: in 2016 (p. 113) she proposes thinking 'seriously about fur-trade colonialism as a distinct and meaningful practice of empire' (Colonial Relations, on the Douglas–Connolly family, is unread). Coordination: the Van Kirk packet (pe139_vankirk) cites Perry 2008 as a later reading; this edge targets the strand, not Van Kirk's entry, to avoid a parallel person-to-person arrow; the editor may re-point it to sylvia_van_kirk if both entries are released together.",
1129:    "note": "Crossref DOI record (Ethnohistory 38:2, 1991, first page 113; last page not given). Only the opening page was read, as the abstract text in OpenAlex; the body is closed. Passages: 'the rather obscure niche occupied by our field, methodology, approach, or whatever it is we do when we do ethnohistory'; 'auto-ethnohistory'; 'a useful handle for some reflections about ethnohistory and ethnohistorians, myself included'; Bill Simmons asked her in August 1988 'if I would be a candidate for president of the American Society for Ethnohistory'. Her predecessors as president, Axtell (1990) and Fogelson (1989), are named."
1249:    "note": "Read in full from the journal's own PDF (galley 1543; scanned, local English OCR; folios 107–110; signed 'Bruce Curtis, Carleton University'). Passages used: 'Anyone who has worked with manuscript census data must be impressed with the meticulous and painstaking analysis undertaken here' (p. 109); 'But I cannot say I enjoyed reading this book. Numbers and narratives do not seem to like one another' (p. 109); 'Two related critical points seem worth making about census artifacts and the matter of gender' (p. 109); the authors 'go so far as to suggest that their cross-sectional study is actually longitudinal' (p. 109); 'The census simply cannot tell us much about the value of distributed property, about the gendered distribution of property, certainly nothing about inheritance patterns, although, as an ideological practice, census-taking attached property to adult men' (pp. 109–110); 'But the book is an important contribution to ongoing debates' (p. 110)."
1537:    "note": "Read in full (pp. 81–94 and the endnotes) in the CIEQ PDF as deposited on depot.erudit.org (dspace bitstream 005921dd; the depot is not the Érudit journal platform and served the file without a challenge); citation, 1997 date and ISBN from the PDF's own 'Pour citer cet article' page. The notes are unpaginated in this version (the PDF moves them after p. 94). Quoted: 'c’est sur Montréal que nous nous appuierons pour illustrer la période qui va de 1850 à 1900. Nous le ferons en utilisant l’exemple local pour ensuite faire ressortir la vue d’ensemble et saisir ainsi, dans une démarche exploratoire, les processus par lesquels la cité se construit'; migration 'Récurrentes de 20 ans en 20 ans' (p. 81); 'Pour la population protestante, notre microéchantillon s’appuie sur dix patronymes' (p. 85); 'parmi les 370 chefs de ménage du Montréal « miniaturisé » de notre échantillon'; trades from the 'rôle d’évaluation' plotted 'à la manière d’une reconstitution cinématographique', in circles every half-kilometre from the Custom House: half the population within one kilometre of the centre in 1861, in the 1–2 km ring in 1881 and the 2–3 km ring in 1901; builders 'telle une vague déferlante, se déplacent vers la périphérie' (p. 89). Note 1: Patricia Thornton (Concordia) is associated 'pour toutes les analyses démographiques' and David Hanna for construction data; 'Ben Johnson et Ingrid Olson ont effectué la cartographie sur ATLAS GIS, et Lilian Lee a fourni l’assistance technique du laboratoire SIG de l’Université McGill'; 'Jason Gilliland a contribué à l’analyse des loyers et de la mobilité'. Note 4: birthplaces of the 1859 baptismal cohort's parents traced through the Drouin marriage index and the Notre-Dame registers."
1985:   "proposal_id": "pe138_kealey_rel_04"
1992:    "context": "Criticised ethnic history that treats ethnicity apart from class and the celebratory histories of 'official ethnic interpreters' that leave out working-class militancy, while calling for cultural analysis of immigrant workers (1981). Optional with edge 7.",
2055:    "context": "Historical sociologist of the census who treats census returns as artefacts of state knowledge: reviewing Darroch and Soltow's study of the 1871 Ontario census (1996) he wrote that 'The census simply cannot tell us much' about how property was valued, gendered or inherited, while calling the book 'an important contribution'; his census history (2001) calls census making 'the making of things to be taken' (as Belshaw quotes it).",
2156:   "new": "Warner’s Boston case links infrastructure and the development of residential space. Olson’s Baltimore (1980) reads the same building in eighteen-year “long swings” of investment.",

exec
/bin/bash -lc "python3 - <<'PY'
import json
p='data/extension-2026/canadian-persons-tier2-2026-10-14/release/editorial-additions.json';d=json.load(open(p))
for n in d['nodes']:
 if n['id']=='sherry_olson':print('WORKS',n['representative_figures_and_works'])
for e in d['edges']:
 if e['proposal_id'] in ['pe139_curtis_rel_02','pe139_harris_rel_07']:print('LABEL',e['proposal_id'],e['map_label'])
PY
sed -n '80,113p' site/field.mjs
rg -n 'metis_representation_criticism|larocque_thesis' historiography-1920-2000.json | head -15" in /home/jic823/historiography
 succeeded in 0ms:
WORKS Sherry H. Olson; The Depletion Myth: A History of Railroad Use of Timber (1971); “Baltimore Imitates the Spider” (Annals of the AAG, 1979); Baltimore: The Building of an American City (1980; bicentennial edition 1997); with Patricia Thornton and Quoc Thuy Thach, “Dimensions sociales de la mortalité infantile à Montréal au milieu du XIXe siècle” (Annales de démographie historique, 1989); “Occupations and Residential Spaces in Nineteenth-Century Montreal” (Historical Methods, 1989); with David Hanna, “The Social Landscape of Montréal, 1901” (Historical Atlas of Canada, vol. 3, plate 30, 1990); “Le peuplement de Montréal” (Atlas historique du Québec: Population et territoire, 1997); with Jason Gilliland, “Claims on Housing Space in Nineteenth-Century Montreal” (Urban History Review, 1998). After 2000: MAP, Montréal, l'avenir du passé (from 2000, with Robert Sweeny and others); “Ethnic Partition of the Work Force in 1840s Montréal” (Labour/Le Travail, 2004); with Gilliland, “Residential Segregation in the Industrializing City” (Urban Geography, 2010); with Gilliland and Danielle Gauvreau, “Did Segregation Increase as the City Expanded?” (Social Science History, 2011); with Patricia Thornton, Peopling the North American City: Montreal, 1840–1900 (2011); with François Dufaux, “Rebuilding a Neighbourhood of Montreal” (2014). Readings: Sweeny (2009); Sager (2012); Horner (2013); Bonnell and Fortin (2014); Lutz, Dunae, Gilliland, Lafreniere and Harvey (2014).
LABEL pe139_curtis_rel_02 The census as artefact, 1996–2001
LABEL pe139_harris_rel_07 Napoleon: a settler perspective, 2004
    start: Math.min(...found.map(f => f[0])), end: Math.max(...found.map(f => f[1])),
    endKind: endsExplicitly ? 'terminus' : continues ? 'coverage_limit' : 'unstated',
    openStart, coverage, precision, curated: false,
  };
}

export function spanOf(node) {
  const d = node.date_span;
  if (d && Number.isFinite(d.start)) {
    const end = Number.isFinite(d.end) ? d.end : null;
    return {
      start: d.start, end: end ?? d.start,
      /* An unverified end is not an ending: continue rather than stop. */
      endKind: end === null || d.end_kind === 'open' ? 'coverage_limit' : (d.end_kind || 'unstated'),
      coverage: Number.isFinite(d.coverage_through) ? d.coverage_through : null,
      precision: d.precision || 'year', curated: true,
      basis: d.basis || '', startKind: d.start_kind || '',
      openStart: ['earlier_roots', 'undated_roots', 'open'].includes(d.start_kind),
    };
  }
  const parsed = parseSpan(node.date_label);
  return parsed || null;
}

/* ---------- journals ---------- */
/* The catalogue is a discovery inventory (1,600+ periodicals). Only journals carrying at
   least one evidenced venue edge become field entries; the rest stay counted, not drawn. */
export function journalNodes(graph) {
  const jc = graph.journal_catalogue;
  if (!jc?.nodes?.length) return {nodes: [], edges: [], unlinked: 0};
  const byId = new Map(jc.nodes.map(n => [n.id, n]));
  const atlas = new Set(graph.nodes.map(n => n.id));
  const edges = (jc.edges || []).filter(e => byId.has(e.source) && atlas.has(e.target));
  const linked = new Set(edges.map(e => e.source));
